{{- /*
JFrog/Artifactory credentials for the NGDE PL2/PL3 bundle builds.
Pulled from 1Password at `chezmoi apply` time; written to ~/.config/jfrog-env (0600).
Sourced by dot_config/shell/env.zsh so JF_USER/JF_API_KEY are always in the environment.

Why a cached file and not `op read` at shell start: two op calls on every new shell is a
noticeable delay, and these are only needed for occasional bundle builds.

The PL3 build scripts (iac/pl3/bundle/build_infrastructure_airgap_bundle.sh and
iac/pl3/scripts/dev_full_infrastructure_flow.sh) hardcode CONFIG_DIR=/tmp and look for
/tmp/jfrog.env. /tmp is tmpfs on WSL, so that file disappears on every restart. Both
scripts fall back to JF_USER/JF_API_KEY from the environment when the file is absent,
which is what this provides. Nothing in the iac repo needs to change.

In 1Password: `username` holds the Artifactory user, `credential` holds the identity token.
The item title contains a "/", which breaks op:// by-name references, so it is addressed
by item ID. Same Automation vault and service/account mode handling as private_dot_ntfy.yml.tmpl.
*/ -}}
{{- $svc := and (hasKey . "op_mode") (eq .op_mode "service") -}}
{{- $item := "op://Automation/k5qjpc4eycg3jjnupi6w66okii" -}}
# Generated by chezmoi from 1Password. Do not edit; run `chezmoi apply` to refresh.
export JF_USER={{ if $svc }}{{ onepasswordRead (printf "%s/username" $item) | quote }}{{ else }}{{ onepasswordRead (printf "%s/username" $item) "LKHBSHPTMJBQLJ36YPLOP6LVLI" | quote }}{{ end }}
export JF_API_KEY={{ if $svc }}{{ onepasswordRead (printf "%s/credential" $item) | quote }}{{ else }}{{ onepasswordRead (printf "%s/credential" $item) "LKHBSHPTMJBQLJ36YPLOP6LVLI" | quote }}{{ end }}
